    verified:
      - "CORRECTNESS, important — the three components this WU built were absent from the app bundle. Adding Field and StatusDot to ui/src/components/index.ts collided with the legacy ui/src/components.tsx, and every screen imports the bare './components', which bundlers resolve to the file over the directory's index. Verified in the built output: dist/assets/index-*.js carried the legacy Field's 'px-3 py-chrome-y' and the bare-button StatusDot, while px-control-x, min-h-control and w-glyph were absent entirely. NOT pre-existing — at BASE the barrel exported neither name, so the specifier had exactly one source; this diff manufactured the collision and then lost to it. Silent by construction: both specifiers are valid, tsc compiled without a word, and all 529 unit tests passed, because they import ./Field and ./index directly and never take the app's resolution path. Fixed in 142a428."
      - "CORRECTNESS, nit — Disclosure's defaultOpen seeds per VALUE, not per mount: React re-applies `open` whenever the prop value changes between renders, so a parent computing it from changing state can reopen a panel the user closed. The shipped test named 'a re-render does not yank a closed panel back open' only exercises the unchanged-value case. Confirmed empirically by probe. Held at nit and NOT fixed: the design's driver ② explicitly wants ManualPanel's `open={state === \"failed\"}` to force the panel open when an action newly fails — the changing-value case working as intended — and Disclosure has no call site until W10. Recorded as deviation D5 in the result and assigned to W10, which is where the tenant arrives and the semantics become answerable against a real screen."
    also_raised:
      - "EFFICIENCY, nit — two Fields and two StatusDots shipping in one bundle. True when raised, and resolved by the same fix: the legacy kit is deleted."
    refuted:
      - "REUSE, important — 'Field should compose Button per DEC-18 rather than repeat its class string.' DEC-18's own canonical example is Select's trigger (Select.tsx:156), which shares the box by matching class string, not by rendering a Button; and Field renders an <input> where Button renders a <button>, so composition is not mechanically available. The retired design's F2 fork stages and settles exactly this question, citing DEC-18 by name."
      - "REUSE, nit — 'enforcing the box parity by a test repeats DEC-18's prose-is-not-a-comparison failure.' Backwards: a machine-checked comparison is the antidote DEC-18 asks for; prose was the disease."
      - "SIMPLIFICATION, nit — 'use Tailwind's group / group-open: instead of the arbitrary-variant selectors.' Tested empirically: `group` is a hard design/vocabulary ERROR under the closed-vocabulary lint (DEC-17), because a marker class that emits no CSS is indistinguishable from a typo. The proposed alternative fails CI. The finder had verified only that it compiles under vanilla Tailwind."
